--TEST--
A child cancelled while it sleeps runs nothing further, and its `catch` never sees the cancellation — `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns` and `rule:concurrency/cancellation-runs-no-user-code`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4: the deadline expires, every child is cancelled, the call waits for
// those cancellations, and `TimeoutError` is thrown. Each child here is
// parked in `Core\Time::sleep` when that happens, which is the case the
// scheduler cannot answer with a forced unwind — the child is standing on a
// `Core` member's `extern "C"` frame — so it is resumed and told instead.
//
// § 5 is the rest of the case: what a told child may run on its way out is
// nothing. The `echo` after the sleep would print if the wait had merely
// ended early, and the `catch` would print if the cancellation were a
// `Throwable`. Neither is, so the whole output is the parent's one line.
try {
    Core\Task::map([1, 2, 3], fn (int $n): int => {
        try {
            Core\Time::sleep(2s);
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            echo "a child caught its own cancellation\n";
        }
        echo "a child ran past its sleep\n";
        return $n;
    }, {deadline: 20ms});
    echo "the deadline was missed\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "the deadline was hit\n";
}
--EXPECT--
the deadline was hit
